WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.020
Working tissue pours out carbon dioxide.

2
00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:12.560
Does that change how tightly blood holds oxygen, or does carbon dioxide just ride

3
00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:13.560
along?

4
00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:19.160
The first film showed blood delivering its drop in saturation, from lung to tissue.

5
00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:23.540
Now ask what makes that drop bigger where tissue is working.

6
00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:30.680
In Copenhagen in 1903, Christian Bohr worked with Karl Hasselbalch and August Krogh.

7
00:00:31.780 --> 00:00:33.280
They took fresh dog blood.

8
00:00:34.333 --> 00:00:39.893
They exposed samples to gas at a set oxygen pressure, and measured how much oxygen

9
00:00:39.893 --> 00:00:44.513
the blood bound. Repeating this at many pressures traces the curve.

10
00:00:45.583 --> 00:00:50.523
Then they raised the carbon dioxide in the gas, and measured again at the same

11
00:00:50.523 --> 00:00:56.323
oxygen pressures. If carbon dioxide simply rode along, nothing should change.

12
00:00:57.603 --> 00:01:02.643
Only carbon dioxide changed, so any difference came from it, or from the acid it

13
00:01:02.643 --> 00:01:03.643
forms.

14
00:01:04.117 --> 00:01:06.837
If something does change, where will it show most?

15
00:01:08.157 --> 00:01:11.037
At a low oxygen pressure, or near full saturation?

16
00:01:17.733 --> 00:01:20.633
More carbon dioxide lowered oxygen binding.

17
00:01:21.853 --> 00:01:26.453
The effect was large at low oxygen pressures and small near full saturation.

18
00:01:27.873 --> 00:01:29.373
The curve had moved to the right.

19
00:01:30.383 --> 00:01:36.403
Today this is called the Bohr effect: a fall in oxygen affinity when pH falls or

20
00:01:36.403 --> 00:01:37.763
carbon dioxide rises.

21
00:01:38.967 --> 00:01:44.067
The shift raises P50, the oxygen pressure at which hemoglobin is half full.

22
00:01:45.267 --> 00:01:47.627
A higher P50 means a looser grip.

23
00:01:48.667 --> 00:01:50.207
Now bring back the bands.

24
00:01:50.207 --> 00:01:54.447
In the lungs, the shifted curve is still nearly full.

25
00:01:54.447 --> 00:01:56.687
Loading barely changes.

26
00:01:57.800 --> 00:02:00.980
But at tissue pressure the shifted curve sits lower.

27
00:02:02.260 --> 00:02:06.320
So the release bracket grows, by about two-fifths at rest in this model.

28
00:02:07.383 --> 00:02:09.243
And that happens where it matters.

29
00:02:10.543 --> 00:02:16.343
Working tissue is high in carbon dioxide and low in oxygen, so blood gives up more

30
00:02:16.343 --> 00:02:17.343
exactly there.

31
00:02:18.217 --> 00:02:24.317
Published in 1904, the paper offered a reason why blood gives up oxygen more readily

32
00:02:24.317 --> 00:02:25.317
in active tissue.

33
00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:29.400
Two other changes also shift the curve right.

34
00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:35.580
Warmer tissue lowers affinity, and working muscle is warmer than resting muscle.

35
00:02:36.940 --> 00:02:40.360
So does more BPG, a small molecule made in red cells.

36
00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:44.200
They are separate effects, each with its own cause.

37
00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:49.600
The name Bohr effect is kept for pH and carbon dioxide alone.

38
00:02:50.733 --> 00:02:54.733
So a modest right shift costs little in the lungs, where blood loads.

39
00:02:55.973 --> 00:02:59.993
It pays out in working tissue, where blood unloads and the need is greatest.
